WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:03.350
The opioid crisis in America is quite severe.

00:00:03.350 --> 00:00:07.760
In 2018, 10.3 million people, ages 12 and
older misused opioids.

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:11.830
Most of these people misuse prescription painkillers,
often turning to the cheaper, more addictive

00:00:11.830 --> 00:00:12.830
alternative, heroin.

00:00:12.830 --> 00:00:17.100
There are 2 types of opioids: endogenous and
exogenous.

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:20.780
Endogenous opioids are neurotransmitters created
and regulated by the body.

00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:25.119
Exogenous opioids are drugs: derived from
plants and produced synthetically.

00:00:25.119 --> 00:00:27.260
These drugs are the source of the modern opioid
crisis.

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:31.980
Both endogenous and exogenous opioids interact
with opioid receptors.

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:36.039
These receptors are coupled with G-proteins
which help modulate neuron signaling.

00:00:36.039 --> 00:00:39.739
One way that the use of opioids can affect
neuron pathways is by preventing neurotransmitter

00:00:39.739 --> 00:00:40.739
release.

00:00:40.739 --> 00:00:43.860
Here’s how it works:
In the presynaptic neuron, when the opioid

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:49.019
drugs bind to an opioid receptor, the G protein
complex splits into two subunits: G-alpha

00:00:49.019 --> 00:00:50.210
and G- beta gamma.

00:00:50.210 --> 00:00:54.670
The G-beta gamma subunit binds to the voltage
gated calcium channel, making them unable

00:00:54.670 --> 00:00:57.499
to open, and blocking calcium ions from entering.

00:00:57.499 --> 00:01:01.319
Neurotransmitter release to the next neuron
is unable to occur because it depends on the

00:01:01.319 --> 00:01:03.929
presence of calcium ions in the presynaptic
neuron

00:01:03.929 --> 00:01:09.300
Exogenous opioids can be extremely dangerous
because unlike endogenous opioids, they are

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:13.720
not moderated and bind to all available receptor
sites blocking crucial pathways between the

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:15.170
brain and respiratory system.

00:01:15.170 --> 00:01:20.100
Every day, an average of 130 Americans die
from opioid overdoses.

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:24.220
Naloxone is a chemical compound that can be
used to block the effects of opioids, saving

00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:25.470
lives in overdose situations.

00:01:25.470 --> 00:01:29.850
It has a similar structure to the opioids
and binds to the same receptor site without

00:01:29.850 --> 00:01:31.890
activating the G protein.

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:35.780
This prevents opioids from binding and keeps
the neuron pathway between the brain and respiratory

00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:39.140
system open, ensuring that people can still
breathe.

00:01:39.140 --> 00:01:41.380
The misuse of opioids can ruin lives.

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:45.659
Some solutions to help fight this crisis are:
To be well educated in the effects, to not

00:01:45.659 --> 00:01:57.750
misuse prescription opioids, and to seek professional
help.

